30 Days of Ansible – Day 3/30

Installing Ansible on Linux: Your First Step Towards Automation

Every automation journey begins with a proper setup. Before writing playbooks and managing servers, you need to install Ansible on your control node.

The good news? Installing Ansible takes just a few minutes.

What is a Control Node?

A Control Node is the machine where Ansible is installed. From this machine, Ansible connects to remote servers over SSH and executes automation tasks.

«Note: Only the Control Node needs Ansible installed. Target servers do not require any Ansible agent.»

Installing Ansible

On Ubuntu/Debian

sudo apt update
sudo apt install ansible -y

On RHEL/CentOS/Rocky Linux

sudo dnf install epel-release -y
sudo dnf install ansible -y

Verify the Installation

Run the following command:

ansible --version

If the installation is successful, you'll see the installed Ansible version along with Python details and the configuration file location.

Real-World Scenario

Imagine you're joining a new DevOps team.

Instead of manually logging into dozens of servers, you:

✅ Install Ansible on your laptop or management server.

✅ Configure SSH access.

✅ Create an inventory file.

✅ Start managing your entire infrastructure from one place.

That's how most production environments use Ansible.

Why Verify the Version?

Knowing your Ansible version helps because:

- Features vary between versions.
- Some modules may behave differently.
- It's the first thing teams check while troubleshooting.

Key Takeaway

Installing Ansible is the foundation of infrastructure automation. Once your Control Node is ready, you're prepared to automate hundreds of servers with just a few commands.
